  {
    "episode_id": 51156,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51156.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 282,
    "turn_text": "I think I already understand it. I don't think it takes a rocket science to understand the position the PGA was in. Sort of the human reaction to try and lash out and try and Again, you're fighting for survival bad point in time. But, you know, lived and go away. They had their first season. They were able to poach, you know, some top players. And, you know, then they went to court. And the legal bills were mounting. And, you know, PGA won some parts of the lawsuit. For example, the Piff was not granted immunity from discovery. And that might have been what triggered the Saudis willingness to sit down and basically end the mutually assured destruction as well. So again, that's what it takes to do a deal. People have to realize it's in their best interest is to stop the lawsuits. And again, I really don't doubt that everybody involved in this negotiation does love the game of golf. They may have different perspectives on how they view the future for goals. How top players ought to be compensated, how many tournaments they need to play in for the good of the game, for the good of themselves. It's a very complex situation. Running a PGA tour outside of you know, live showing up was difficult enough. I'm sure challenging enough. And then all of a sudden you have, you know, an entity that wants a foothold that wants to say in how professional golf is managed with virtually unlimited resources or willing to spend, you know, way out spend you, that's a real challenge. So I mean, I can kind of understand what the management of the PGA tour was going through. I also understand why they had to keep these negotiations very closely held. I think, you know, there's a lot of uproar from players initially. I think just as you read accounts, some of them have calmed out a little bit as they kind of realize, yeah, you couldn't really tell like a dozen players and not tell the rest of us. So, again, they're in the midst of a negotiation. And I think this has, you know, This will not be easy to conclude. Let's put it this way that, you know, especially now it's been made public and their people attacked you from all sides. Um, so I hope they can come to agreement. I hope they can repair the breach. A few things on that. I think,",
